{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA (debug-protocol Phase 1, analysis-only) — root cause PROVEN by local stubbed-transport experiments (no Jira). IMPORTANT CONTEXT FIRST: the production fix has ALREADY LANDED on origin/main as commit 30c522cde9 under [rebar:ab7f-f0cc-7384-43a7] (closed), whose message states 'Closes the production half of cabc-7a98-d173-4d7c'. This ticket therefore looks ready for verification + close by its owner; evidence below.\n\nCONFIRMED ROOT CAUSE (pre-fix tree = parent of 30c522cde9). All three whole-project readers in src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_graph.py — get_parent_map (loop at :218), get_comment_map (:319), get_issuelinks_map (:413, pre-fix line numbers) — walked POST /rest/api/3/search/jql with a hand-rolled cursor loop whose ONLY exits were 'isLast' truthy or a null/absent 'nextPageToken'. No same-token-twice guard and no request ceiling: a server that repeats the same non-null nextPageToken with isLast falsy makes every request byte-identical, neither break fires, and the loop never terminates. Second-order defect: each loop's fail-open 'except Exception: log WARNING; break' converts the eventual transport failure into a silently PARTIAL whole-project map — the same silent-truncation class as change 1105, [rebar:9263-b404-cb6c-463f], [rebar:deac-4f5a-856a-49bd]. The guard already existed in fetcher._iter_pages (fetcher.py:293-306, SilentTruncationError reason='same-token-twice'); these readers are a separate cursor path and lacked it.\n\nDISCRIMINATING EVIDENCE (runtime, _direct_rest_post_json stubbed):\n- E1, pre-fix readers vs a stub returning {issues:[1 issue], nextPageToken:'same-token-forever'}: each of the 3 readers issued 26 requests, stopped ONLY by the harness cap at 25, swallowed the harness error at WARNING ('REST search failed ... degrading gracefully'), and RETURNED a 1-entry map — reproduces this ticket's measurement exactly (26 requests, 1-entry map).\n- E2 kills the rival hypothesis 'the client fails to echo the cursor, so the server legitimately repeats': recorded bodies show request 1 carries no token and requests 2+ all echo nextPageToken='same-token-forever' byte-identically. The echo is correct; the defect is purely the missing stall guard.\n- E3 proves the changeable artifact: HEAD readers against the SAME stub stop at exactly 2 requests and raise RunawayPaginationError PAST the fail-open handler (nothing swallowed). Same server, one variable — the guard — flips the behavior as predicted.\n- E4: held-out suite green on HEAD: 42 passed in tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_acli_graph_pagination_heldout.py, including test_a_repeated_cursor_token_stops_the_walk_on_its_own, now a plain assertion (:367).\n\nLEGITIMACY: defect — behavior violated the pre-existing strict-xfail oracle and the design invariant already enforced in fetcher._iter_pages, with three prior closed bugs in the same class.\n\nAC STATUS ON HEAD (all four met): (1) all three readers route through the single shared _iter_cursor_pages (acli_graph.py:41-88). (2) a stalled cursor is LOUD — it raises the dedicated RunawayPaginationError (acli_graph.py:80-86) rather than fetcher's SilentTruncationError(reason='same-token-twice'); intent (loud, no partial map) is met and a distinct exception for the distinct cursor protocol is defensible — recording that deviation here since the AC named the fetcher exception. (3) the fail-open handlers no longer convert a stall into a partial map: explicit 'except RunawayPaginationError: raise' arms at acli_graph.py:304-307, :394-397, :477-480. (4) the xfail is converted to a plain passing assertion.\n\nSIBLING CANDIDATES across the other paginated readers (named, not filed):\n- jira_datacenter/transport.py::_paged_search (:379-416) — CONFIRMED at runtime as a live sibling of the class: it terminates ONLY on an empty batch (:412) with no repeated-page/offset-stall guard, so a DC server that ignores startAt and re-serves the same non-empty page spins forever — my stub probe issued 26 calls and ended only at the 25-call harness cap, exactly like pre-fix acli_graph. fetcher._iter_pages carries offset-stall detection for precisely this; DC's pager does not, and test_dc_transport_pagination_heldout.py has NO stall/runaway coverage (no offset-stall / same-token / runaway hits). Second-order twin: DC get_parent_map (:515) wraps it in 'except Exception' (:547) returning {} silently; get_issuelinks_map (:418) and get_comment_map (:432) call it unwrapped. Note [rebar:ab7f-f0cc-7384-43a7] deliberately scoped this pager OUT as stable and pinned by bug 9263's structural gate — but that pin covers short-page/advance-by-returned semantics, not a stall guard; the stall hole is real and unpinned.\n- fetcher.py::_iter_pages (:288) — clean; it is the reference implementation (same-token-twice + offset-stall guards).\n- adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py::_parse_paginated_comments (:517) / get_comments --paginate — not a rebar-driven cursor loop (pagination happens inside the ACLI binary; the parser iterates a finite string). No spin risk in rebar code.\n\nPHASE-2 SKETCH: for THIS ticket no production work remains — verify 30c522cde9 against the ACs (done above) and close referencing ab7f. The regression RED oracle already exists and has teeth: removing the token-equality guard at acli_graph.py:80 sends test_a_repeated_cursor_token_stops_the_walk_on_its_own red (E1 vs E3 demonstrated exactly that flip). For the DC sibling, if picked up: add a stall guard inside _paged_search mirroring fetcher._iter_pages semantics (repeated FULL page = raise a loud stall error; repeated SHORT page = clean stop), and the suggested RED oracle is a test in test_dc_transport_pagination_heldout.py driving _paged_search with a startAt-ignoring stub client, asserting it self-terminates within 2 calls with a loud error instead of hitting a harness call cap."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785797829784238001,"uuid":"07ca2c1c-d507-46fd-818e-9978ebc854ea"}